            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00120-013-3314-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Recent developments and innovations in modern radiation oncology enable radiation oncologists to deliver a biologically, physically and technically stratified individualized treatment. In urological oncology, radiotherapy can be offered as a valid treatment alternative for a number of tumors, such as prostate cancer or urothelial cancer/bladder cancer.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">By improving the precision of radiotherapy, significant improvement in tumor control can be achieved, coupled with a reduction of the risk of treatment-related side effects. Techniques such as high precision radiotherapy and combinations of radiation and modern imaging, such as image-guided radiotherapy (IGRT) and adaptive radiotherapy (ART), make daily individually tailored treatment possible.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In the future the biological characteristics of tumors and of normal tissue as well as molecular markers can be used for treatment stratification.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
